# This file records source mathematics that is not yet implemented in the
# executable Litex chapters. It is intentionally comment-only and is not
# exported by litex.config.

# Chapter 1 — Introduction; source definition FermatLastTheorem and theorem
# hard.
# Missing mathematics: state the natural-number theorem that no nonzero
# x, y, z satisfy x^n + y^n = z^n for n > 2, and supply a checked proof rather
# than the source's admitted illustration. This is a full number-theory
# development, not an executable assumption.

# Chapter 5, Section 5.1 — Irrational roots and prime multiplicity.
# The callable prime-factor exponent and all named source theorems are present.
# The remaining mathematics is existence of the exponent function, its product
# and power laws, and proofs of the coprime-square and exponent-divisibility
# conclusions currently stated with trust.

# Chapter 5, Section 5.2 — MyNat.
# The separate carrier, constructors, induction interface, recursive addition
# and multiplication, computation equations, and eight named theorems are
# present. Their genuine inductive construction and structural-induction
# proofs remain to replace the localized trusts.

# Chapter 5, Section 5.3 — Primes congruent to 3 modulo 4.
# Both source theorem statements are present. The strong-induction
# factorization proof and the Euclid-style unboundedness proof remain.

# Chapter 6, Section 6.1 — finite_product_ne_zero.
# The exact theorem is present; its finite-set induction proof remains.

# Chapter 6, Section 6.2 — triangle_card and double_counting.
# The triangle carrier and both theorem statements are present. The finite
# counting proofs, including the incidence-sum double count, remain.

# Chapter 6, Section 6.3 — lists, binary trees, and propositional formulas.
# All source-facing carriers, constructors, recursive callables, equations, and
# named theorems are present. Generic recursion/induction constructions and the
# structural proofs currently represented by trust remain to be supplied.

# Chapter 7, Section 7.2 — permutation group.
# The equivalence operations, nonempty carrier, and callable Group object are
# present. The remaining proof is the exact Group record membership, especially
# associativity as equality of composed function records.

# Chapter 7, Section 7.3 — Gaussian integers.
# The callable commutative-ring and Euclidean-domain objects, centered integer
# operations, Gaussian quotient/remainder/rank, and source-facing laws are
# present. Coordinate ring laws, centered decomposition and bound, Gaussian
# remainder estimate, and the final Euclidean-domain law proof remain.

# Chapter 8, Section 8.1 — hierarchy, scalar actions, and modules.
# Ring addition commutativity is proved, and the self-module, natural/integer
# scalar actions, integer module, and product additive monoid are callable.
# Generic recursion, module membership, and componentwise product laws remain
# to replace their localized trusts.

# Chapter 8, Section 8.3 — quotient monoid.
# The projection, nonempty quotient carrier, quotient multiplication and
# representative equation, identity, and callable Monoid object are present.
# Projection membership, representative independence, associativity, and
# identity proofs remain.

# Chapter 9 — Groups and Rings; source group normalization, homomorphism,
# subgroup, finite-group, and conjugation results.
# The source-facing normalization, homomorphism, subgroup-closure, Lagrange,
# Sylow, subgroup-cardinality, coprime-intersection, and conjugation statements
# are present. Missing mathematics: replace their localized trusts with the
# inverse-uniqueness, closure, counting, and finite-group proofs.

# Chapter 9 — Groups and Rings; source permutation group, free group, and
# presented-group constructions.
# Callable free-group and presented-group carriers, generators, and universal
# lifts are present. Missing mathematics: construct reduced words, relation
# quotients, inverse permutations, and prove their law and computation
# packages instead of the localized trusts.

# Chapter 9 — Groups and Rings; source actions, orbit-stabilizer, cosets, and
# quotient-group theorems.
# Cayley injectivity, orbit-stabilizer, quotient-group carrier, projection,
# operations, Group object, representative multiplication, and universal lift
# are present. Missing mathematics: discharge the action/counting,
# representative-independence, group-law, lift, and first-isomorphism trusts.

# Chapter 9 — Groups and Rings; source generic ring identities, units, quotient
# rings, ideal products, first isomorphism, and Chinese remainder theory.
# Unit multiplication, inverse selection, Group membership, and the restriction
# of ring homomorphisms to units are checked. Quotient-ring operations and
# CommutativeRing, ideal product, first-isomorphism map, and Chinese maps are
# callable. Missing mathematics: prove the quotient well-definedness,
# ideal-product, kernel/range, and finite CRT law packages.

# Chapter 9 — Groups and Rings; source polynomial construction and results.
# X, constants, addition, multiplication, powers, evaluation, degree, and roots
# are callable. X, constants, and pointwise addition now have checked
# finite-support constructions. Missing mathematics: implement convolution,
# powers, finite evaluation, and degree selection and replace the degree/root
# trusts; composition, natural degree refinements, and the bivariate circle
# proof remain absent.

# Chapter 10 — Linear Algebra; remaining source vector-space constructions.
# The linear-equivalence inverse and its two inverse equations are checked, as
# is pointwise composition. Binary product structure, pairing/cpairing
# linearity, and singleton-supported direct-sum inclusions are checked. Indexed
# products, finite-support direct sums, and a universal lift are present.
# Missing mathematics: implement the finite-sum universal lift, add the
# structured scalar-to-endomorphism map, dependent heterogeneous carriers,
# projections, parallel product map, and finite-index equivalence.

# Chapter 10 — Linear Algebra; source kernel, range, quotient-space, and first-
# isomorphism results.
# The quotient carrier, its nonemptiness, the typed canonical projection, and
# surjectivity of that projection are established. Missing mathematics:
# selected inherited-subspace and quotient carriers, VectorSpace objects,
# projections, compatible lift, first-isomorphism map, correspondence map, and
# internal-direct-sum decomposition are present. Missing mathematics: connect
# selected refined carriers definitionally to the displayed subspaces/cosets
# and replace their operation, linearity, decomposition, equivalence, and
# correspondence trusts.

# Chapter 10 — Linear Algebra; source polynomial endomorphism theory.
# Polynomial evaluation on endomorphisms, coprime-kernel intersection,
# minimal/characteristic polynomial objects, and Cayley-Hamilton are present.
# Missing mathematics: implement the finite evaluation and polynomial
# selections, replace their theorem trusts, add kernel-join decomposition, and
# prove the root/eigenvalue equivalences.

# Chapter 10 — Linear Algebra; source matrix inverse and basis-coordinate
# constructions.
# General multiplication, determinant, inverse, coordinates, and change-of-
# basis functions are callable. Missing mathematics: implement their finite
# sums and selections, prove inverse/reconstruction/change-of-basis laws, and
# add Basis.mk/constr, canonical bases, toMatrix, and determinant invariance.

# Chapter 10 — Linear Algebra; source dimension results.
# A finite-dimensional space selects its dimension, index set, and basis.
# Missing mathematics: replace these selection trusts and prove positivity,
# finite-index equivalences, sum/intersection formulas, subspace bounds, and
# equinumerosity of arbitrary basis index sets.

# Chapter 11 — Topology; source filter laws and real-filter limits.
# Principal, atTop(N), map, and comap filter-law promotions, both Tendsto
# formulations, and real addition/scalar limit laws are checked. Missing
# mathematics: prove map/comap composition and identify the real at-point
# filter. Chapter 11 contains no executable trust or axiom declaration.

# Chapter 11 — Topology; source metric-space analysis.
# Continuous distance/quadratic composition, closed sequence limits and
# closure, compact universe/closedness, reverse anchored Cauchy,
# geometric-step convergence, uniform continuity on compact sets, Baire, and
# its closed-cover/contains-a-ball consequence are checked. Missing
# mathematics: prove `closed_unit_interval_metric_compactness_goal` from real
# completeness or Bolzano–Weierstrass, then prove
# `compact_metric_continuous_extrema_goal` from real order completeness. These
# are non-executable goal propositions, not theorem facts.

# Chapter 11 — Topology; source induced/coinduced, separation, extension, and
# sequential compactness results.
# The induced/coinduced constructions and comparison, changed-topology
# continuity iff, homogeneous Pi topology, Hausdorff uniqueness, regular/open
# bases, first-countable closure/sequence equivalence,
# cluster-point characterization, filter compactness, compact subsequences and
# images, mapped cluster points, and finite indexed subcovers are checked.
# Missing mathematics: prove `dense_subset_continuous_extension_goal` using a
# checked global selection interface and Hausdorff uniqueness/continuity of
# the selected limits, and support the fully dependent Pi carrier. The
# dense-extension goal is not published as a theorem fact.

# Chapter 12 — Differential Calculus; source selected derivative and elementary
# derivative theorems.
# The total derivative interface, sine/pi background, sine, sum, fifth-power,
# and sine-at-pi examples are present with localized trusts. Missing
# mathematics: construct the background objects and replace their theorem
# trusts.

# Chapter 12 — Differential Calculus; source extrema, Rolle, and mean value
# theorems.
# Zero derivative at a local minimum, Rolle, and mean value are present with
# localized trusts. Missing mathematics: carry out their one-sided,
# extreme-value, and auxiliary-function proofs.

# Chapter 12 — Differential Calculus; source norm and continuous-linear-map
# examples, finite-dimensional completeness, operator norm, and uniform
# boundedness.
# Norm nonnegativity, the norm triangle inequality, and continuous-linear-map
# add/smul projections are checked. The remaining named norm laws, induced
# metric, norm continuity, continuous-linear-map continuity, callable operator
# norm, its two characteristic
# inequalities, pointwise/uniform family bounds, natural-number level sets,
# the shell estimate, and the real-scalar Banach–Steinhaus theorem are present.
# Missing mathematics: replace the localized continuity/supremum,
# completeness-transport, closed-level-set, Archimedean-cover, recentering, and
# shell-to-operator-norm trusts, and transfer completeness through a finite
# basis.

# Chapter 12 — Differential Calculus; source selected Frechet derivatives,
# higher derivatives, and inverse function theorem.
# A callable selected Fréchet derivative and its identification theorem are
# present. Missing mathematics: prove derivative uniqueness and construction,
# construct multilinear iterated derivatives and finite-order smoothness, and
# carry out the quantitative local-inverse construction with its two inverse
# laws and inverse derivative.

# Chapter 13 — Integration and Measure Theory; source elementary integration.
# Callable oriented and whole-line integrals, logarithm, reciprocal, real
# convolution, the identity/reciprocal formulas, and both fundamental theorems
# are present. Missing mathematics: construct the analysis-library objects and
# replace the localized integral and FTC trusts.

# Chapter 13 — Integration and Measure Theory; source extended nonnegative
# reals and ENNReal-valued measure results.
# The elementary measurable-space closures, generic countable additivity, AE
# family, and AE/eventually equivalence are present. Missing mathematics:
# construct ENNReal with zero, infinity, order, infinite sums, infima, and
# conversion to real; specialize the generic measure interface; and prove
# measurable-superset infimum and countable-subadditivity results.

# Chapter 13 — Integration and Measure Theory; source Bochner integration and
# major integration theorems.
# Missing mathematics: construct strongly measurable and integrable function
# carriers, Bochner and set integrals and their basic laws, product measures and
# product integrals; prove dominated convergence and Fubini; and develop the
# Jacobian, convolution, and change-of-variables results.
